const ANALYSIS = `<h1>The Graveless Doll of Eric Mutis — A Close Reading</h1>
<h2>The Sentence-Level Story</h2>
<p>Karen Russell's story operates at the level of specific words, specific echoes, specific moments of diction where a single pronoun or a botanical inventory or a parenthetical confession does more argumentative work than any thematic summary can account for. The meaning lives in the Monday morning sentence, in the straw, in the rabbit, in the backseat of a green Cadillac, in the blood on Larry's palms that he calls "ours." The themes emerge from those details rather than being stated separately from them. A close reading of this story has to work at the level of the sentences, because that's where Russell put the argument.</p>
<h2>The Shared Raw Material</h2>
<p>Larry and Eric are built from the same substance. Russell establishes this quietly, through accumulation rather than announcement, planting the parallels across the first half of the story and trusting you to assemble them.</p>
<p>Larry describes himself as "a quiet kid, branded mean." Eric is quiet, branded mutant. Larry can't say his own last name — he pronounces it "Rubby-oh," flattening the Spanish into something rubbery and diminished, a sound his own mother can't replicate. Eric's name gets deformed past recognition into Mutant, Mucus, the Mute. Both boys are on the free lunch program. Both live in poverty — Larry in a shitbox apartment on First and Stuckey in ear-splitting proximity to the hospital, Eric in a house that's "a zombie zone of grime and confusion." Both have absent or damaged paternal figures — Larry's father left when he was two and Larry holds the man's surname like Wile E. Coyote holding the handle of an exploded suitcase, latching into pure space; Eric has the cadaverous man in the green Cadillac whose relationship to him Russell will never clarify, only shadow. Both boys have private interior lives they hide from the world — Larry loves Music I "better than pot" but stands in the back making a Clint Eastwood seam with his lips and singing only in his mind; Eric has Saturday, his rabbit, his whispered language behind the cage bars, the face that changes until he isn't ugly anymore.</p>
<p>The raw material is the same. The same silence, the same stillness, the same outsider energy. What differs is the machinery around it. Larry's silence gets absorbed into Camp Dark — into the "we," into the collective, into the four-body organism that manufactures quiet through violence — and the absorption converts his outsider identity into armor. Eric's silence, unabsorbed, becomes the thing the armor gets tested on. Without Camp Dark, without Gus and Mondo and Juan Carlos, without the blacktop and the Science Building and the whole infrastructure of belonging that comes with the group, Larry's quietness might have become vulnerability instead of menace. He might have been Eric. The scarecrow makes that impossible to ignore.</p>
<p>But the mirroring is not perfect, and the imperfection is important. Eric seems genuinely unbothered in a way Larry never is. Larry is always watching himself, always sick with himself, always aware of what he's doing and unable to stop doing it. Eric just is. He wears the same Hoops sneakers every Monday. He takes the beatings with drifting blue eyes. He can't be taught to be ashamed of himself because he doesn't accept the premise that he should be. Larry's self-awareness is constant and corrosive — he knows the distance between him and Eric is circumstantial, knows it in his body before he knows it in his mind, and the knowing is what produces the guilt and the guilt is what produces the story but the guilt never once prevents the violence. Eric has something Larry doesn't, which is the capacity to refuse the framework of shame, and that capacity — that weird calm — is what enrages the bullies more than cowering ever could.</p>
<h2>Russell's Method: The Straw</h2>
<p>The structural spine of the story is not the plot. It's the straw.</p>
<p>Three scenes, three encounters with the same material, the same words recurring in the same order across the length of the narrative. The first time: Larry standing in the park, looking down at the scarecrow's innards — "This straw, I recognized with a chill, was fresh and green." The sentence does almost nothing on a first read. Texture, unsettling detail, the wrongness of living straw inside a dead thing. Larry's body goes cold and the sentence moves on and you move on with it.</p>
<p>The second time: Eric's bedroom. Saturday's cage. "Pine straw. Timothy, orchard, meadow. Alfalfa — plus calcium!" The straw gets named, gets given its source — a rabbit's bedding, a child's tenderness, the botanical inventory of the one thing Eric loves.</p>
<p>The third time: the Cone, at night, at the end. Larry crouching over what's left of the scarecrow — "it was rabbit bedding, I thought. Timothy, meadow, orchard. Pine straw."</p>
<p>The language is nearly identical across all three scenes. Russell is connecting the park to the bedroom to the Cone through diction alone, threading the scarecrow's body back to the living boy's most intimate possession, and she's trusting the reader to feel the connection before they can explain it. The first time you read the straw you don't know what you're reading. The second time you recognize it. The third time you understand it. The entire story is built around that arc of recognition, and the arc works because Russell plants the detail early, lets it sit inert, and then detonates it retroactively. The meaning was always there. You just didn't know what you were looking at — the same way Larry felt the chill in the park and didn't know what he was feeling, the recognition arriving in his body before it arrived in his mind, the straw already telling him everything he would spend the rest of the story trying to understand.</p>
<p>But here's the thing — this is Larry's recognition, not an objective fact Russell states. Larry sees the straw in the Cone and thinks "rabbit bedding." Larry hears the botanical names and connects them to the bedroom. The connection is made by a narrator who has spent weeks not eating, not sleeping, vomiting, confessing, dreaming about Eric, imagining himself lashed to the oak eating bloodred straw. Either Larry is right and the straw confirms that Eric built the scarecrow from the material of his own love, or Larry is so consumed by guilt that he's finding Eric in everything he looks at, projecting the bedroom onto the park because he can't stop looking for the boy he helped destroy. Russell keeps the question open. She gives you the identical language across the three scenes — "Timothy, meadow, orchard" — but she gives it to you through the eyes of an unreliable narrator, a boy who needs the straw to mean something because the alternative is that the scarecrow is just a scarecrow and his guilt has nowhere to go. The echo might be Russell stitching two scenes together deliberately. The echo might be Larry's mind stitching them together compulsively. Both readings work. Both are supported by the text. And the impossibility of resolving which one is true is part of what makes the straw the structural spine of the story rather than just a recurring detail — it carries the full weight of the question the story is built around, which is whether Eric's presence in Larry's life is real or manufactured by the same guilt that manufactured everything else.</p>
<p>This is Russell's method throughout: meaning that accrues retroactively, details that change what they mean when you encounter them a second time in a different context. The straw is the clearest instance but the whole story operates this way — the parenthetical about the red-sewn mouth, the Wile E. Coyote handle, the mirror in the bathroom, the pronoun shift from "your blood" to "our blood." Each one plants a seed in a scene that seems to be about something else and lets the seed germinate across pages until it blooms in a scene that recontextualizes everything that came before. Reading this story once gives you the plot. Reading it twice gives you the argument. The argument lives in the echoes.</p>
<h2>Saturday and the Sealed Interior</h2>
<p>Eric's rabbit is named after the best day of the week. The one day with no school, no obligations, no performance required, no audience demanding that Eric be Mutant or Mucus or the Mute. Saturday the rabbit is completely unbothered — she wrinkles her nose into "a joyless, princessy expression, as if breathing air were an onerous obligation," she drinks from her water nozzle and ignores Eric's adoration with magnificent indifference, she doesn't perform anything for anyone. She is Eric's private interior life made physical, the self that nothing at school can touch or humiliate, the self that exists only in the bedroom, only behind the closed door, only in the space between Eric's whisper and the cage bars.</p>
<p>Larry can't hear what Eric is saying to the rabbit. It's a private language that exists completely outside everything that happens to Eric in the world — the beatings, the name-calling, the shape in the hallway, the hallway itself. None of it exists in whatever Eric is whispering. "Behind the cage bars his whole face was changing. Mutant kept changing until he wasn't ugly anymore." The ugly was never in Eric's face. It was in how they looked at him. When the looking stops — when the only audience is a rabbit who doesn't care — Eric's face becomes something Larry has never seen before, and the never-having-seen is its own indictment. All those months in the same school, the same hallways, the same blacktop, and Larry never once saw Eric's actual face because the looking was always framed by the group and the group only had one way to look at him.</p>
<p>Eric has fortified the bedroom with rabbit supplies to an irrational degree — fifty-pound bags of straw and food pellets in every corner, "turned his room into a bunny stronghold." More food than one rabbit could eat in months, as if the room might need to sustain itself indefinitely, as if the door might need to stay closed for a very long time. A child building a sanctuary inside an unsafe house. The bedroom is the one clean, loved space in an apartment where the furniture rears on its side and the appliances crouch and a hunched shape flows past the door into the bathroom. Everything outside the bedroom is the Cadillac driver's domain — grime, confusion, objects that look like they've been struck. Everything inside it is Saturday's domain — bright water bottle, exotic straw, a cage that gleams like Incan treasure in the dust. The contrast between the two spaces is the contrast between what's happening to Eric and what Eric has managed to protect. The fifty-pound bags are the fortifications of a kid who knows that what he loves can be taken from him and is building walls out of the only material he can afford.</p>
<h2>The Bedroom and the Monday Morning Sentence</h2>
<p>The bedroom scene is the hinge of the entire story. Eric opens every door available to him: takes Larry in after the bike accident with no acknowledgment of what Larry has done to him at school, lends him a sweater, shows him Saturday, shares the stolen rabbit secret — the Miss Molly posters in the drawer, the girl named Sara Jo, the whole story of how he found a rabbit and kept it and pulled down every poster and built his life around this one small stolen warmth. He guides Larry's hand into the cage. He makes a joke — "Peaches, that's just fine" — the first and last joke he makes in the entire story. He gets quiet with Larry. The whole world gets quiet and the only sound is the rabbit drinking water and for a minute or two, catching their breath, they get to be humans together.</p>
<p>"Some hard pressure flew away from me like air out of a zigzagging balloon." Something genuinely cracks open in Larry. The Camp Dark identity requires an audience — Gus, Mondo, Juan Carlos, the blacktop, the whole architecture of who-Larry-is-at-school — and without the audience the identity loses its structure and what's left is just a kid in a borrowed sweater feeling the hum of a living thing under his palm. "Under my palm, a cache of white life hummed." Two boys touching the same animal in the same direction. For thirty seconds the entire architecture of their relationship dissolves.</p>
<p>Then:</p>
<p>"I never returned Mutant's sweater, and the following Monday I did not speak to him."</p>
<p>One sentence. Not dramatized. Not narrated. Not given a scene or a paragraph or even a moment of decision — if there even was a decision, if it was even a choice rather than a gravitational return to the default, the Camp Dark identity reasserting itself the way a body reasserts its posture after being pushed. Russell doesn't show you Larry walking into homeroom and looking away. She doesn't narrate the withdrawal. The flatness is the point — the withdrawal is so ordinary it doesn't even merit description. It's one sentence, stated in the same tone you'd use to mention forgetting to return a library book, and then Eric disappears.</p>
<p>"I hid the cuts on my palms in two fists." He's still carrying the wound from the glasses. He hid it, which means he looked at it, which means he knows what happened and chose to hide the evidence and walk back into the school and close every door Eric had opened.</p>
<p>Within a week Larry calls Sara Jo. Using his fake elderly voice — the "one-hundred-year-old voice" he uses to scam pizza discounts — he reaches into the one sealed space Eric had and empties it. One phone call. He calls twice to confirm the rabbit is gone. The fake voice is a performance of power, a costume, the Camp Dark identity in its purest form — taking something from someone smaller because taking is what the identity requires. The version of Larry that petted the rabbit and felt the white life hum can't make this phone call. The two Larrys are incompatible. He destroys the bridge before anyone can see he crossed it.</p>
<p>After Saturday is taken, Eric's eyes go frozen. "Permafrost around each pupil. Two telescopes fixed on a lifeless planet." Permafrost — permanent frost, ice that doesn't thaw. Something has closed in Eric that won't reopen. The rabbit was the last thing keeping him present, the last tether between Eric and the capacity to be reached, and Larry cut it. The story is essentially over for Eric at this point even though he's physically present for a few more weeks. He tried once more — "Larry — ," he started to say in the bathroom — and Larry wrung his hands in the sink and walked out, "following Mutant's example and avoiding our faces in the mirror." They never looked at each other again and then one day Eric was gone.</p>
<p>The saddest moment in the story is not the beatings or the stolen rabbit or the scarecrow in the Cone. It's the moment when something else was completely possible and didn't happen, and Russell tells you it didn't happen in one flat sentence and moves on.</p>
<h2>What Russell Won't Narrate</h2>
<p>The Cadillac driver is old, cadaverous, liver-spotted, smoking a cigar. He almost kills Larry with his car and keeps driving. Eric goes bright red when Larry asks about him — father? grandfather? uncle? mom's boyfriend? — and won't answer. Eric, who endures everything at school with that weird calm, who takes the beatings and the name-calling without visible distress, who can't be embarrassed by anything they do to him — this same Eric can't meet Larry's eyes when asked about the man in the Cadillac.</p>
<p>Always the backseat. Never the passenger seat. The detail implies cargo, not companion. Russell calls the driver "a hunched shape" when he passes the bedroom door — not a man, a shape, deliberately dehumanized, fluid rather than solid, something that "flows past." Eric is on his feet before Larry hears the hinge, closing the door on "ballerina toes" with a speed and silence that's clearly practiced, clearly not the first time, the body performing its defensive choreography without conscious thought.</p>
<p>Juan Carlos saw Eric in Vice Principal Derry's office through the window. Derry caught him looking and slammed the blinds. Eric looked "bad. Like, scared? Worse than he did when we messed with him." That comparative — <em>worse</em> — does enormous work. The school beatings are violent and sustained. For them to register as the <em>lesser</em> fear, for whatever is happening in Derry's office to produce a version of scared that surpasses what Camp Dark can produce, the thing behind the blinds has to be categorically different from the thing behind the Science Building.</p>
<p>Russell never explains what happened in Derry's office. She never explains the Cadillac driver's relationship to Eric. She never narrates what the hunched shape does after it flows past the bedroom door. She gives you the blinds slamming, the backseat, the bright red face, the ballerina toes, the furniture rearing on its side, and she trusts you to feel the shape of what she's refusing to say. The refusal is deliberate. The school violence is public, witnessed, finite — it begins and ends and leaves marks Larry can see. What's happening at home has no witnesses, no endpoint, and Eric can't name it when directly asked. Russell won't narrate it because Eric can't narrate it. The silence in the text mirrors the silence in the boy.</p>
<h2>Eric's Calm: Damage or Dignity</h2>
<p>"His ugliness panned into a weird calm, and this combination was like a bully allergen." There are two readings of that calm:</p>
<p>Eric's calm is damage. Whatever is happening with the Cadillac driver has already exhausted his capacity for alarm. School violence registers as continuation — a second weather system moving through a landscape the first already flattened. His non-resistance isn't courage. It's the stillness of someone for whom the distinction between harm and its absence has lost its meaning. He doesn't flinch because flinching requires a baseline of safety to flinch from, and Eric doesn't have one.</p>
<p>Eric's calm is refusal. He genuinely doesn't accept the framework that makes his Hoops sneakers shameful, his Medicaid glasses a provocation, his body a thing that needs correcting. They smash his glasses and he returns Monday in an identical pair. They steal his sneakers and he comes back in the same nine-dollar Hoops from the Starmart box. The repetition isn't obliviousness — it's the quiet assertion that their judgment doesn't govern him. He won't change because he doesn't recognize their power to require change.</p>
<p>The story won't resolve which reading is true because both are necessary. The damage reading explains why Eric doesn't resist — he's been conditioned past the point where resistance is available to him. The dignity reading explains why the violence never stops — his calm functions as a refusal to validate their authority, and without validation the violence has no catharsis, no endpoint, no proof that it's working. Cowering would end it. Tears would end it. Agreement would end it. Eric offers none of these. Every Monday he returns unchanged and every Monday he passively wins something none of them can name, and the not-naming is what keeps the machine running. The two readings coexist the way Eric's two selves coexist — the school self whose eyes are "drifting, unplugged" and the bedroom self whose face changes behind the cage bars until he isn't ugly anymore. Both are real. Neither cancels the other. The ambiguity is the character.</p>
<h2>The Machinery of the "We"</h2>
<p>Camp Dark is not a friendship. It's a machine.</p>
<p>"In fact, as a 'we,' Camp Dark was pretty fiercely uninterested in the details of its members' lives outside of school or beyond the fenced urban woods of Friendship Park." Nobody asks about anyone's life beyond the park. Nobody names the bruises on Mondo's arms, the cancer, the alcoholic aunt, Juan Carlos's possibly pregnant sister. "Silence policed the shady meeting point under our oak." The collective identity is built on mutual non-disclosure — you are admitted to the group on the condition that you bring nothing with you from outside it, no private pain, no family wreckage, no vulnerability that might breach the shared surface. The silence is the price of membership and the price is worth paying because inside the silence you are safe, you are armored, you are part of a "we" that can manufacture the calm none of you can access alone.</p>
<p>The manufacturing is literal. "We were like four factory guys, manufacturing the quiet." The word turns violence into industry, cruelty into labor, four boys beating a fifth boy behind the Science Building into a production line. No perpetrators in a factory. No victims. Just workers and a product. Larry can describe the beatings without calling them beatings because the language of labor absorbs the language of the fist — they are producing something, meeting a demand, the demand is for quiet, and so the violence becomes necessary manufacture, and the necessity is what lets Larry live with it.</p>
<p>But the product is Eric's silence. Eric the Mute. They are literally producing his muteness, beating the sound out of him week by week until one day he disappears, and the disappearance is the final product, the completed unit. The factory built a boy's absence and called it calm.</p>
<p>Russell distributes the group's anatomy across four bodies, each one representing a different component of how collective cruelty sustains itself:</p>
<p>Gus is the executor. He carries his mother's deboning knife without anyone knowing and uses it to cut the scarecrow down and afterward sits back next to his beer as if they're at the beach. The cruelty costs him nothing — no guilt, no sleeplessness, no vomiting, no confession. He turns everything into a joke or an experiment. He is the most dangerous of the four not because he's the most violent but because the violence leaves no residue in him. Whatever mechanism converts violence into guilt in Larry doesn't exist in Gus, or was disabled so long ago that it no longer registers.</p>
<p>Juan Carlos is the rationalizer. He does homework, corrects Mondo's confusions, speaks with precision — "to absorb the risk of a lawsuit," he says about the playground foam, and the language is its own armor. When Larry starts confessing private things — his mother's name, that he can't say his own last name — Juan Carlos says "Nobody cares. Now you pull yourself together." That's the group's immune system responding to a breach. His clarity is a defense mechanism. He represents the part of the organism that intellectualizes everything to avoid feeling it.</p>
<p>Mondo is complicity that knows itself. Bruises on his arms mentioned once, home life implied unhappy, reactions that keep "missing the mark entirely and slamming into a non sequitur, as if his rage were a fierce and stupid bird that kept landing on the wrong tree." His smile trembles when Larry says he didn't dismantle the scarecrow. He knows something is actually wrong. "When Gus wasn't around, Mondo became smarter, kinder, and more afraid." That sentence is the anatomy of group cruelty in miniature — the cruelty requires the group, the kindness surfaces only when the group dissolves, the fear is what's there all along underneath both. Mondo goes with Larry to the Cone at the end. He's terrified. He goes anyway. He's not innocent but he's reachable, and his fear is the only honest thing any of them are feeling.</p>
<p>Larry is the conscience that doesn't prevent anything. He feels everything and does it anyway. He knows the distance between himself and Eric is circumstantial. He knows the straw is rabbit bedding. He knows the cuts on his palms are "our" blood. He keeps wanting to bury the scarecrow, keeps going to the park alone, keeps not eating, keeps vomiting, keeps confessing. The self-awareness makes him the most culpable in a certain sense, not the least — Gus doesn't know what he's doing, or doesn't care, and the not-knowing is its own kind of innocence. Larry knows. He has the vocabulary to describe his own cruelty with precision while performing it — "credibly asseverate," a legal word, a twelve-year-old wiping blood on his sleeve and reaching for the stiffest, most formal vocabulary available to him, bureaucratizing violence into procedure, turning assault into paperwork. The language itself is performing the cover-up. And the intelligence that produces the language is the intelligence that understands the full dimensions of what's happening, and the understanding doesn't stop the fist, not once, not even after the bedroom, and the gap between understanding and stopping is the thing Larry can't close and the thing the scarecrow won't let him forget.</p>
<h2>The Scarecrow: Memorial and Mirror</h2>
<p>The boys keep asking what the scarecrow is protecting Friendship Park from. Rabid cats? Mob gunmen? Sewer rats? They never get an answer because they're asking the wrong question.</p>
<p>The scarecrow isn't protecting the park from something external. It's protecting Larry from his own capacity for forgetting. "Courteously, the kid had disappeared from my brain entirely, about the same time he vanished from our school rolls." Larry had successfully made Eric not exist. The forgetting was complete and comfortable — Eric vanished from school, vanished from Larry's mind, and the vanishing felt like courtesy, like Eric was politely removing himself. The scarecrow makes that impossible. It is Eric's absence made physically present, impossible to ignore, a memorial planted in the exact place designed for forgetting — the park where Larry goes to "send jets of inky thoughts into the Cone," where the whole point is to not-think, to not-remember, to expulse the year's facts in spasms of summer amnesia.</p>
<p>The scarecrow is stuffed with Saturday's straw. Eric knew Camp Dark — the inscription on the oak, ERIC MUTIS / SATURDAY inside a heart, carved on the sunless side of the tree, confirms he'd been there privately. If Eric built the scarecrow — and the evidence accumulates toward this — the construction is a kind of authorship. He made a doll of himself from the material of his own love and placed it in the territory of the boys who hurt him, on the tree they carved their names into, on the side they never looked at. He took the thing they would recognize — his face, his clothes, his height — and built it into something they couldn't ignore or explain or make go away.</p>
<p>The dismemberment follows a specific grammar. Hoops sneakers taken first — the same sneakers the boys stole from Eric at school. Then hands, then arms, then legs. If Eric is removing the limbs himself, he's restaging his own victimization on his own effigy in the place where the people who hurt him will have to watch it happen. The scarecrow's body replays what they did to Eric's body. The audience is forced to watch. The audience can't intervene. And the audience blames Larry — the one member whose guilt is actually real — which means Larry is being punished for the right thing by people who think they're punishing him for the wrong thing. Larry says "I didn't do it" and he's right about the physical limbs and completely wrong about everything else. He did do it. He did all of it. The glasses, the rabbit, the Monday morning silence. His friends are right for the wrong reasons and Larry can't defend himself because any defense would require admitting the truth.</p>
<p>Russell preserves the ambiguity deliberately. If Eric is removing limbs he has to be alive. But the damage sounds large — "big enough to tear an arm off at the root." Animals? Something else? Maybe Eric started it and something continued. The ambiguity is the point. Eric was never allowed a clear, legible existence while present. His absence doesn't owe them clarity either.</p>
<p>Larry proposes to become "the scarecrow's scarecrow" — to stake himself at the park and stand guard over what's left, to catch whatever is feeding on the doll. The phrase is almost a thesis statement for the whole story. A scarecrow's scarecrow would be a real human imitating a scarecrow imitating a human — a recursive loop where the copy of the copy has more reality than the original. And that's what's happening. The doll version of Eric is more present, more demanding, more morally weighted than the living Eric ever was. The boys paid more attention to the scarecrow in two weeks than they paid to Eric Mutis in an entire school year. Larry proposing to take the scarecrow's position means proposing to become what Eric was — lashed to the tree, standing watch, protecting rather than destroying. He even dreams it: subbing himself in for Eric on the oak, eating "horsey fistfuls of a bloodred straw." His friends refuse. They won't go. That refusal is part of why Larry ends up descending into the Cone alone.</p>
<h2>The Librarian and the Systems That Fail</h2>
<p>One adult intervenes. Once. Mrs. Kauder, the librarian — she walks over like "a leopardess, flaunting all her bones" and says "that is a no-no" and calls them by their real names, gently, "as if she were breaking a spell." She sends each boy to his next class and takes Eric with her and for one moment the system works. An adult sees what is happening and names it and stops it and the stopping has authority behind it.</p>
<p>And then it just continues. There is no second conversation. No consequence. No sustained attention. The librarian goes back to her carrels and the boys go back to the blacktop and Eric goes back to the weeds behind the Science Building. Larry says they needed the librarian to keep reading them "her story of our lives, her good script of who we were" for every minute of every day — and the need is real, the observation is precise, the boys genuinely required an external voice narrating them as something better than what they were — "but of course she couldn't do this, and we did get lost."</p>
<p>Russell's parenthetical about the librarian — "this librarian was a forty-year veteran of her carrels and I think that light was going out" — is quietly devastating. Forty years of watching children hurt each other and naming it "a no-no" and sending everyone to their homerooms and the naming has never been enough to make the violence stop. The person who saw it most clearly is the person whose capacity for hope is failing. She represents what one adult noticing without sustained action actually accomplishes, which is that the violence pauses long enough for the adult to walk away and then resumes.</p>
<p>The institutions fail everywhere. The guidance counselor can't find Eric — the district computers were wiped by a virus. The yearbook slot reads ABSENT in red letters. Vice Principal Derry shuts the blinds. Coach Leyshon sneers "Pick it up, Mutant!" on the courts. The phone books across America produce dozens of apologetic Mutises and no Erics. Every system that might have preserved Eric or tracked his disappearance has either failed or been complicit, and the failures are not dramatic — they're bureaucratic, procedural, the quiet failures of institutions that were never designed to notice a kid like Eric Mutis in the first place.</p>
<h2>The Descent and the Insufficient Gesture</h2>
<p>Larry buys a nameless dwarf rabbit for nineteen dollars at the mall. Not Saturday. He knows returning Saturday would "appease or solve nothing" — the word "appease" implying something that needs to be placated, some force consuming the scarecrow that operates beyond rational explanation. He's moved from Hypothesis 1 (a human is doing this) through Hypothesis 2 (animals) to Hypothesis 3 (Something Else) to Hypothesis 4, the one he can barely articulate: "I think we made him." Not stitched him. Not built him. Made the conditions. Made the necessity. Made the scarecrow inevitable through what they did to the boy.</p>
<p>The rabbit is Larry's only available language for apology. He can't find Eric. He can't return Saturday. He can't undo the beating or the glasses or the Monday morning silence. A nineteen-dollar rabbit from the mall pet store is all he has.</p>
<p>Mondo goes with him. He's terrified — sits on the playground donkey and says "I am not going" and his face looks tumescent and red, "as if his cheeks were swelling preemptively to protect him." But he goes. He holds the rope. He calls down "it was OK, I was OK" when Larry slips and swings out from the rock wall, and Larry says "I don't think it's possible to overstate the love I felt in that moment for Mondo Chu." The statement is raw and unguarded in a way Larry almost never allows himself to be. Fear shared is survivable. That's what Mondo gives Larry in the dark. That's what Larry never gave Eric.</p>
<p>Larry descends into the Cone — the place where they threw their garbage, where Eric's effigy was pushed, the ravine that was always their trash can and is now an underworld. "Fireflies dotting the lacunae between its frozen roots like tiny underworld lights." He crouches over what's left of the scarecrow and sees the straw for what it is, finally, rabbit bedding, Timothy, meadow, orchard, the recognition completing its circuit. He releases the substitute rabbit and it roots into the straw and disappears, "first went with its furry ears, its bunching back, the big, velour skis of its feet." A new life entering the relic of the old one.</p>
<p>Then Larry stands with his arms stretched wide. The posture of the scarecrow on the oak. The posture of crucifixion. The scarecrow's scarecrow, finally realized. "I felt as if the black sky was my body and I felt as if the white moon, far above me, unwrinkled and shining, was my mind." The sentence reaches — the most poetic Larry gets, the most desperate for his experience to mean something beyond what it literally is — and the reaching doesn't quite land, and the not-quite-landing is part of what Russell is doing. The gesture is real. The feeling is real. The insufficiency is also real. It doesn't fix Eric. It doesn't undo the Monday morning or the phone call or the glasses or the years. A kid standing in a ravine full of trash with his arms out, guarding a rabbit that isn't Saturday over a scarecrow that isn't Eric — Russell is honest that the gesture is not enough without being cruel about the not-enough.</p>
<h2>Graveless</h2>
<p>"Somewhere I think I must still be standing, just like that."</p>
<p>The final line shifts to present tense. The story has been told in past tense from the beginning — everything remembered, everything narrated from a distance — and the shift at the very end means the Cone is not a place Larry left. It's a place he's still in. The Larry narrating and the Larry standing in the dark with his arms out collapse into the same person, the same moment, the same posture of protection that can't protect anything because there's nothing left to protect.</p>
<p>The title: <em>The Graveless Doll of Eric Mutis.</em> Graveless. Not a grave. No burial. No record in school computers — wiped by a virus. No trace in phone books — dozens of apologetic Mutises, no Erics. Yearbook slot: ABSENT in red letters. The scarecrow is the only memorial Eric gets, and even that gets consumed by something in the night until nothing is left but straw blowing in a ravine full of condoms and chip bags and beer bottles and the accumulated waste of four boys who treated a person as disposable and disposed of him.</p>
<p>Whether Eric is alive or dead, the story won't say. Both possibilities are bleak in different registers. If he's dead — the scarecrow appearing after his disappearance, "graveless" in the title, the Cadillac driver, the house, the blinds slamming, Larry compulsively imagining him dead — then the story is about a boy who was killed by the accumulated weight of everything that happened to him, at school and at home, and nobody noticed because the systems that might have noticed were too broken or too complicit to function. If he's alive — somewhere completely alone and untraceable, carrying everything that happened to him with nobody who knows his name — that's its own kind of death, a social and institutional erasure so complete that the living and the dead versions of Eric are indistinguishable from the outside.</p>
<p>Either way: nobody knows. The not-knowing is part of Larry's punishment. He never tried to find out when it mattered. He had one night in Eric's bedroom, one chance to see who Eric actually was, and he went back to school on Monday and closed every door and called Sara Jo and took the rabbit and walked out of the bathroom and the walking-out was repeated, was habitual, was performed so many times that it became the only thing Larry knows how to do — walk away from Eric Mutis, walk back into Camp Dark, walk back into the "we" — and now Eric is gone and the walking-away can't be undone and Larry is standing in the Cone with his arms out, forever, in a posture of protection over a scarecrow that is already nothing, guarding what he helped to destroy, and the standing is the only thing he has left and the standing is not enough and the story ends.</p>`;
